{
  "teamId": 142,
  "year": 2022,
  "narrative": "The 2022 Minnesota Twins entered the season with genuine optimism, built around a healthy Byron Buxton and the blockbuster signing of Carlos Correa. April validated that hope almost immediately, with Buxton launching towering home runs and Correa delivering clutch hits that signaled this team could compete in the AL Central. A 12-9 April gave way to an even stronger May at 18-13, the best month of the season, as the Twins rode their offense and a solid rotation to sit near the top of the division.\n\nJune is where the cracks began to show. The bullpen, which would haunt this team all year, started leaking badly — Jharel Cotton surrendered a devastating two-run homer to Josh Naylor that swung nearly a full win in probability, and a brutal final weekend of June against Cleveland saw Tyler Thornburg give away multiple leads in a single series. Still, the Twins entered July with a competitive record, and Buxton's 21st home run on July 1st offered a reminder of the ceiling this roster possessed when everything clicked.\n\nJuly and August were a tale of treading water. The team went 10-12 and 14-14 in those months, never pulling away and never fully collapsing. Moments of genuine brightness — Nick Gordon's grand slam in late August, Jake Cave's timely single, Gio Urshela's clutch home runs — were offset by bullpen implosions and defensive miscues that refused to let this team build momentum. Jorge López, acquired at the trade deadline to help shore up the back end, struggled in his new role and gave up key runs in critical spots.\n\nSeptember was where the season died. A 10-18 collapse, punctuated by errors, blown leads, and late-inning heartbreaks, knocked the Twins out of playoff contention. Pitchers like Trevor Megill and Dereck Rodríguez could not hold games together, and a team that had once looked like a division contender finished 78-84, six games below .500. The promise of Buxton and Correa, ultimately, could not overcome a bullpen that ranked among the most damaging in baseball when the pressure was highest.",
  "chapters": [
